Tests invoke the function directly with concrete arguments matching the
declared schema. Only when from_step is declared, replace that reference
with entries containing the actual records; no scratchpad exists in a test.
Do not invent argument names or a from_step value. For an invalid item in a
best-effort batch, check fail_count_eq and error_contains; ok=false is for
an invalid invocation, such as a non-list where a list is required.

Produce 4-6 tests with a unique name, object input and nonempty expect.
Available matchers: ok, ok_count_eq, fail_count_eq, error_contains.
MUST: use only name, input and expect, with at least one expectation.
MUST NOT: emit setup, teardown, env, reference, shell commands or dependencies on host network, keys or data.
OK: {"name":"empty_list","input":{"entries":[]},"expect":{"ok":true,"ok_count_eq":0}}.
ERROR: preparing or cleaning up files through shell, even with an empty string.

Each case gets a fresh private environment without network: fixture/input.txt contains "birth fixture\\n", fixture/empty.txt is empty and fixture/output is an empty directory. Use paths relative to this environment when files are needed; other inputs are concrete data. Host files are unavailable.

Required cases: one item, empty list when applicable, invalid arguments and a domain edge case.

Shape-only example for change_texts; do not copy names, input or expectations literally.
{
  "tests": [
    {
      "name": "one_item",
      "input": {
        "entries": [
          "Ab"
        ]
      },
      "expect": {
        "ok": true,
        "ok_count_eq": 1
      }
